    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "A habit, or something that has not happened yet",
     "texto": [
      "For a situation that **repeats**, use the **indicative**:\n\n- **Cuando tengo** tiempo, leo.\n\nFor a **future** action that has not happened yet, use the **subjunctive** after **cuando** or **en cuanto**:\n\n- **Cuando tenga** tiempo, te llamaré.\n- **En cuanto llegue**, te escribo.\n\nThe other part of the sentence can be in the **future** (te llamaré), in the **present with future meaning** (te escribo) or in the **imperative** (llámame).\n\nIf the event already happened, you go back to the indicative: **cuando llegué**, llovía."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "Cuando + indicative or subjunctive",
     "texto": [
      "With **cuando** and **en cuanto** choose the mood according to the time:\n\n- **Habit**: indicative. Cuando llego a casa, leo un rato.\n- **Past**: indicative. Cuando llegué a casa, llovía.\n- **Future** (it has not happened yet): subjunctive. Cuando llegue a casa, leeré un rato.\n\nThe main verb can be future, present or imperative: cuando llegues, **llámame**.\n\nA trick: if you can add «todavía no» and the sentence still makes sense, use the subjunctive."
     ],
